When two 2 port networks are connected in parallel, i.e. in a manner such that the net input and output currents are the sum of the input and output current of each port and input and output voltages of each port are equal, the equivalent parameters are obtained by addition of their individual Y parameters. For Network 1, \[\begin{bmatrix}
    I_1\\I_2
\end{bmatrix}=\begin{bmatrix}
    y_{11}&amp;y_{12}\\y_{21}&amp;y_{22}
\end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}
    V_1\\V_2
\end{bmatrix}\] For Network 2, \[\begin{bmatrix}
    I_1'\\I_2'
\end{bmatrix}=\begin{bmatrix}
    y_{11}'&amp;y_{12}'\\y_{21}'&amp;y_{22}'
\end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}
    V_1'\\V_2'
\end{bmatrix}\] Now since \(V_{1eq}=V_1=V_1'\) ,\(V_{2eq}=V_2=V_2'\), \(I_{1eq}=I_1+I_1'\) and \(I_{2eq}=I_2+I_2'\), therefore the equivalent admittance parameters include : \[\begin{bmatrix}
    I_{1eq}\\I_{2eq}
\end{bmatrix}=\begin{bmatrix}
    I_1\\I_2
\end{bmatrix}+\begin{bmatrix}
    I_1'\\I_2'
\end{bmatrix}\] Thus, \[\begin{bmatrix}
    I_{1eq}\\I_{2eq}
\end{bmatrix}=(\begin{bmatrix}
    y_{11}&amp;y_{12}\\y_{21}&amp;y_{22}
\end{bmatrix}+\begin{bmatrix}
    y_{11}'&amp;y_{12}'\\y_{21}'&amp;y_{22}'
\end{bmatrix})\begin{bmatrix}
    V_{1eq}\\V_{2eq}
\end{bmatrix}\] Hence, \[[Y_{eq}]=\begin{bmatrix}
    y_{11}&amp;y_{12}\\y_{21}&amp;y_{22}
\end{bmatrix}+\begin{bmatrix}
    y_{11}'&amp;y_{12}'\\y_{21}'&amp;y_{22}'
\end{bmatrix}\] \[\boxed{(c) \textit{y-parameters}}\]

For MOSFET devices: <ul><li>Electron mobility in N-channel MOSFET is higher than hole mobility in P-channel MOSFET.</li><li>Hence N-channel MOSFETs switch faster.</li><li>Due to higher mobility, they provide better current driving capability.</li><li>Noise immunity and TTL compatibility are not the deciding advantages here.</li></ul> \[\boxed{\begin{array}{l}
\text{(B)}\quad \text{It is faster}\\
\text{(D)}\quad \text{It has better drive capability}
\end{array}}\]

Since all the capacitors are large, the BJT base is at AC ground. Hence, the MOSFET operates in common-source configuration and the BJT operates in common-base configuration, forming a cascode stage. The drain current of the MOSFET is, \(i_d=g_{m(\text{MOS})}v_i\) The collector current of the BJT is, \(i_c=\alpha i_e\approx\alpha i_d\), where \(\alpha=\dfrac{\beta}{\beta+1}
=\dfrac{99}{100}
=0.99\) Therefore, the overall transconductance is, \[g_m=\alpha g_{m(\text{MOS})}
=0.99\times2
=1.98\,\text{mA/V}\] \[\boxed{(D)\;1.98\,\text{mA/V}}\]

For a Darlington pair, the overall current gain is: \(h_{FE}=h_{FE1}h_{FE2}+h_{FE1}+h_{FE2}\) Given, \(h_{FE1}=h_{FE2}=100\) Therefore, \(h_{FE}
=(100)(100)+100+100
=10200\) \[\boxed{(D)\;10200}\]

Since the coupling and bypass capacitors are very large, they act as short circuits for AC analysis. From the given small-signal model, \(h_{re}=0\). Hence, \(I_b=0\). Therefore, the controlled current source is, \(h_{fe}I_b=0\) The output impedance is the parallel combination of the transistor output resistance and the collector resistance. \[r_o=\frac{1}{h_{oe}}
=\frac{1}{0.05\,\text{mmho}}
=20\,\text{k}\Omega\] \[Z_o
=r_o\parallel R_C
=20\,\text{k}\Omega\parallel5\,\text{k}\Omega
=\frac{20\times5}{20+5}
=4\,\text{k}\Omega\] \[\boxed{(D)\;4\,\text{k}\Omega}\] <img src="img/2c2cc785ea09895d.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,608px)">
